### Instruction:
Create a Java program for solving cubic equations with complex roots. The program should handle both real and complex coefficients. Additionally, implement a user interface that allows the user to input the coefficients and displays the roots of the equation.

### Response:
Here is a Java program that solves cubic equations with complex roots and includes a user interface:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class CubicEquationSolver {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Cubic Equation Solver");
        System.out.println("---------------------");

        System.out.print("Enter the coefficient of x^3: ");
        double a = scanner.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the coefficient of x^2: ");
        double b = scanner.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the coefficient of x: ");
        double c = scanner.nextDouble();
        System.out.print("Enter the constant term: ");
        double d = scanner.nextDouble();

        Complex[] roots = solveCubicEquation(a, b, c, d);

        System.out.println("\nRoots:");
        for (int i = 0; i < roots.length; i++) {
            System.out.println("Root " + (i + 1) + ": " + roots[i]);
        }
    }

    public static Complex[] solveCubicEquation(double a, double b, double c, double d) {
        Complex[] roots = new Complex[3];

        double delta0 = b * b - 3 * a * c;
        double delta1 = 2 * b * b * b - 9 * a * b * c + 27 * a * a * d;

        if (delta0 == 0 && delta1 == 0) {
            // Three equal real roots
            double root = -b / (3 * a);
            roots[0] = new Complex(root);
            roots[1] = new Complex(root);
            roots[2] = new Complex(root);
        } else if (delta1 * delta1 - 4 * delta0 * delta0 * delta0 > 0) {
            // One real root and two complex roots
            double C = Math.cbrt((delta1 + Math.sqrt(delta1 * delta1 - 4 * delta0 * delta0 * delta0)) / 2);
            double D = Math.cbrt((delta1 - Math.sqrt(delta1 * delta1 - 4 * delta0 * delta0 * delta0)) / 2);

            roots[0] = new Complex((-b + C + D) / (3 * a));
            roots[1] = new Complex((-b - (C + D) / 2) / (3 * a), (C - D) * Math.sqrt(3) / (6 * a));
            roots[2] = new Complex((-b - (C + D) / 2) / (3 * a), -(C - D) * Math.sqrt(3) / (6 * a));
        } else {
            // Three real roots
            double C = Math.cbrt(Math.sqrt(delta1 * delta1 - 4 * delta0 * delta0 * delta0));
            double D = Math.atan2(Math.sqrt(4 * delta0 * delta0 * delta0 - delta1 * delta1), delta1);

            roots[0] = new Complex((-b + 2 * Math.sqrt(delta0) * Math.cos(D / 3)) / (3 * a));
            roots[1] = new Complex((-b - Math.sqrt(delta0) * (Math.cos(D / 3) + Math.sin(D / 3) * Math.sqrt(3))) / (3 * a));
            roots[2] = new Complex((-b - Math.sqrt(delta0) * (Math.cos(D / 3) - Math.sin(D / 3) * Math.sqrt(3))) / (3 * a));
        }

        return roots;
    }

    static class Complex {
        private double real;
        private double imaginary;

        public Complex(double real, double imaginary) {
            this.real = real;
            this.imaginary = imaginary;
        }

        public Complex(double real) {
            this.real = real;
            this.imaginary = 0;
        }

        public double getReal() {
            return real;
        }

        public double getImaginary() {
            return imaginary;
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            if (imaginary == 0) {
                return String.valueOf(real);
            } else if (real == 0) {
                return imaginary + "i";
            } else if (imaginary < 0) {
                return real + " - " + Math.abs(imaginary) + "i";
            } else {
                return real + " + " + imaginary + "i";
            }
        }
    }
}
```

You can compile and run the program to use the user interface for solving cubic equations with complex roots. The program prompts the user to enter the coefficients of the cubic equation and displays the roots. Complex roots are represented in the form "a + bi", where "a" is the real part and "b" is the imaginary part.

