### Response:
To insert a comment in CSS code that is not visible when compiled or displayed in a browser, you can use CSS media queries along with the CSS `display` property. To restrict the visibility of the comment to specific users with a certain permission level, you can wrap the comment inside a media query condition that targets those users.

Here's an example code snippet:

```css
div {
    margin: 0 auto;
    width: 50%;
    text-align: center;
}

/* Comment only visible to users with permission level 2 or higher */
@media (min-width: 0) {
    /* Insert comment here */
    div {
        display: none;
    }
}
```

In the above code, the media query `@media (min-width: 0)` has a condition that will always evaluate to true. This ensures that the comment is included in the CSS file. However, the `div` selector inside the media query sets the `display` property to `none`, effectively hiding the comment from being displayed in the browser.

To make the comment visible to specific users with a certain permission level, you can modify the media query condition. For example, if users with permission level 2 or higher have a CSS class named "user-level-2", you can update the media query condition like this:

```css
@media (min-width: 0) and (.user-level-2) {
    /* Insert comment here */
    div {
        display: none;
    }
}
```

This will make the comment visible only to users with the "user-level-2" class applied to their elements.

