### Response:
Here is a C# program that creates a list of ten strings, each containing at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one special character. The program then prints out each string, with all uppercase letters replaced by lowercase letters and vice versa:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        List<string> strings = new List<string>
        {
            "Str1@",
            "STR2#",
            "sTr3$",
            "string4%",
            "sTRIng5^",
            "STRING6&",
            "strinG7*",
            "String8(",
            "sTrING9)",
            "sTRINg10!"
        };

        foreach (string str in strings)
        {
            string modifiedStr = ModifyString(str);
            Console.WriteLine(modifiedStr);
        }
    }

    static string ModifyString(string str)
    {
        char[] charArray = str.ToCharArray();

        for (int i = 0; i < charArray.Length; i++)
        {
            if (char.IsLower(charArray[i]))
            {
                charArray[i] = char.ToUpper(charArray[i]);
            }
            else if (char.IsUpper(charArray[i]))
            {
                charArray[i] = char.ToLower(charArray[i]);
            }
        }

        return new string(charArray);
    }
}
```

This program uses a `List<string>` to store ten example strings. The `ModifyString` function is used to replace all uppercase letters with lowercase letters and vice versa. The `foreach` loop is then used to iterate over each string in the list and print the modified strings.

