### Instruction:
Write a Java class named "Shape" which has an abstract method named "surfaceArea". Additionally, the "Shape" class should have a constructor that takes in a parameter representing the dimensions of the shape. The dimensions can be of any type, such as integers or floating-point numbers. The "Shape" class should also have a method named "volume" which calculates and returns the volume of the shape based on its dimensions. The volume calculation should be specific to each type of shape (e.g., a cube would have a different volume calculation compared to a sphere). The subclasses of the "Shape" class should implement the "surfaceArea" and "volume" methods based on their specific shape type.

### Response:
Here is the Java code for the "Shape" class and its subclasses:

```java
abstract class Shape {
    protected Object dimensions;

    public Shape(Object dimensions) {
        this.dimensions = dimensions;
    }

    public abstract double surfaceArea();

    public abstract double volume();
}

class Cube extends Shape {
    public Cube(int sideLength) {
        super(sideLength);
    }

    @Override
    public double surfaceArea() {
        int sideLength = (int) dimensions;
        return 6 * sideLength * sideLength;
    }

    @Override
    public double volume() {
        int sideLength = (int) dimensions;
        return sideLength * sideLength * sideLength;
    }
}

class Sphere extends Shape {
    public Sphere(double radius) {
        super(radius);
    }

    @Override
    public double surfaceArea() {
        double radius = (double) dimensions;
        return 4 * Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double volume() {
        double radius = (double) dimensions;
        return (4 / 3) * Math.PI * radius * radius * radius;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape cube = new Cube(5);
        System.out.println("Cube Surface Area: " + cube.surfaceArea());
        System.out.println("Cube Volume: " + cube.volume());

        Shape sphere = new Sphere(2.5);
        System.out.println("Sphere Surface Area: " + sphere.surfaceArea());
        System.out.println("Sphere Volume: " + sphere.volume());
    }
}
```

In this code, the "Shape" class is an abstract class with an abstract method "surfaceArea". It also has a constructor that takes in the dimensions of the shape. The subclasses of "Shape" (e.g., "Cube" and "Sphere") implement the "surfaceArea" and "volume" methods based on their specific shape type.

In the "Cube" subclass, the surface area and volume calculations are specific to a cube. In the "Sphere" subclass, the surface area and volume calculations are specific to a sphere.

In the "Main" class, we create instances of the "Cube" and "Sphere" subclasses and demonstrate how to calculate the surface area and volume of each shape.

