### Response:
You can achieve this by using CSS and the `calc()` function to dynamically set the radius of the circle based on the viewport width using media queries. Here's a code snippet to accomplish that:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      .circle {
        width: 50%; /* Adjust the width of the circle as per your requirement */
        height: 0;
        padding-bottom: 50%; /* Maintain a 1:1 aspect ratio */
        background-color: blue;
        border-radius: 50%;
      }

      @media (max-width: 600px) {
        /* Set the radius to 40% of the viewport width for small screens */
        .circle {
          width: calc(40vw);
          padding-bottom: calc(40vw);
        }
      }

      @media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1200px) {
        /* Set the radius to 30% of the viewport width for medium screens */
        .circle {
          width: calc(30vw);
          padding-bottom: calc(30vw);
        }
      }

      @media (min-width: 1201px) {
        /* Set the radius to 20% of the viewport width for large screens */
        .circle {
          width: calc(20vw);
          padding-bottom: calc(20vw);
        }
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="circle"></div>
  </body>
</html>
```

In the above code snippet, the `.circle` class sets the width, height, and background color to create a circle using `border-radius: 50%`. By adjusting the `width` property and maintaining a 1:1 aspect ratio with `padding-bottom`, the circle's size will be determined based on the viewport width.

The `@media` queries are used to set different radius values for different screen sizes. In this example, the circle's radius is set to 40% of the viewport width for small screens (max-width: 600px), 30% for medium screens (min-width: 601px and max-width: 1200px), and 20% for large screens (min-width: 1201px). You can modify these values according to your specific requirements.

