### Instruction:
Create a CSS animation to apply an interesting effect to a given element. The animation should only be triggered when the user hovers over the element, and it should include at least three different keyframes. Additionally, the animation should have a duration of 5 seconds and should loop infinitely.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an example of a CSS animation that applies a rotating effect to an element when the user hovers over it. It includes three different keyframes and has a duration of 5 seconds with an infinite loop:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        /* Define the animation */
        @keyframes rotate {
            0% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
            50% { transform: rotate(180deg); }
            100% { transform: rotate(360deg); }
        }

        /* Apply the animation to the element */
        .element {
            width: 100px;
            height: 100px;
            background-color: red;
            animation: rotate 5s infinite;
        }

        /* Trigger the animation on hover */
        .element:hover {
            animation-play-state: running;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="element"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, the `@keyframes` rule defines the animation called `rotate`, which rotates the element by 360 degrees. The animation has three keyframes: at 0%, the element is rotated by 0 degrees, at 50%, it is rotated by 180 degrees, and at 100%, it is rotated by 360 degrees.

The `.element` selector applies the animation to the element, sets its width and height, and gives it a red background color. The `animation` property sets the animation to `rotate`, with a duration of 5 seconds and an infinite loop.

Finally, the `.element:hover` selector triggers the animation to play when the user hovers over the element by setting `animation-play-state` to `running`.

