### Instruction:
Rewrite the CSS styling for the HTML structure in Task 2 to make the webpage responsive for different screen sizes.

### Response:
To make the webpage responsive for different screen sizes, you can use media queries in CSS. Below is an example of how you can modify the CSS styling in Task 2 to achieve a responsive design:

```css
/* Default styling for all screen sizes */

body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.container {
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 20px;
}

h1 {
  text-align: center;
}

p {
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

/* Responsive styling for smaller screen sizes */

@media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
  .container {
    padding: 10px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 24px;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 14px;
  }
}

/* Responsive styling for even smaller screen sizes */

@media screen and (max-width: 480px) {
  .container {
    padding: 5px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 18px;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 12px;
  }
}
```

In the above code, we first define the default styling for all screen sizes. The `.container` class sets a maximum width and centers it horizontally. The `h1` and `p` tags have default styles.

We then use media queries to apply different styles for smaller screen sizes. In the first media query, for screens up to 768px width, we reduce the padding in the `.container`, decrease the font size for `h1`, and reduce the font size for `p`.

In the second media query, for screens up to 480px width, we further reduce the padding, decrease the font size for `h1`, and reduce the font size for `p`.

These media queries allow the webpage to adapt its styling based on the screen size, providing a responsive layout for different devices. You can modify these styles according to your specific needs.

