### Instruction:
Create a responsive web page with a CSS style to hide all paragraphs in the HTML body on screens larger than 768px. The web page should have a button that, when clicked, reveals the hidden paragraphs only on screens larger than 768px. Additionally, the hidden paragraphs should fade in smoothly with a duration of 1 second.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an example of how you can create a responsive web page with a CSS style to hide paragraphs on screens larger than 768px. When you click the button, the hidden paragraphs will fade in smoothly with a duration of 1 second.

HTML code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <style>
        /* Hide paragraphs on screens larger than 768px */
        @media (min-width: 769px) {
            p {
                display: none;
            }
        }

        /* Button style */
        .reveal-button {
            display: none;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            cursor: pointer;
        }

        /* Show button on screens larger than 768px */
        @media (min-width: 769px) {
            .reveal-button {
                display: block;
            }
        }

        /* Fade in animation */
        .fade-in {
            opacity: 0;
            transition: opacity 1s ease-in;
        }

        .fade-in.show {
            opacity: 1;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <button class="reveal-button" onclick="revealParagraphs()">Show paragraphs</button>
    <p class="fade-in">Paragraph 1</p>
    <p class="fade-in">Paragraph 2</p>
    <p class="fade-in">Paragraph 3</p>

    <script>
        function revealParagraphs() {
            var paragraphs = document.querySelectorAll('p.fade-in');
            for (var i = 0; i < paragraphs.length; i++) {
                paragraphs[i].classList.add('show');
            }
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Explanation:
- In the CSS code, the media query `@media (min-width: 769px)` is used to hide paragraphs on screens larger than 768px by setting their `display` property to `none`.
- The button with the class `reveal-button` is hidden by default using `display: none;`. However, inside the media query, it is set to `display: block;` to show it on screens larger than 768px.
- The `fade-in` class is applied to the paragraphs for the smooth fade-in effect. It sets the initial opacity to 0 and adds a transition effect to the `opacity` property with a duration of 1s.
- The JavaScript function `revealParagraphs()` is triggered when the button is clicked. It selects all paragraphs with the class `fade-in` and adds the `show` class to them, which sets their opacity to 1, resulting in a fade-in effect.

