How can an HTML element be moved to the bottom right corner of a page, while ensuring it stays fixed in that position even when the page is scrolled? Additionally, the element should only be visible within a specific viewport height and width range, and should dynamically adjust its size and position when the browser window is resized.

### Response:
To achieve the desired positioning and behavior, you can use CSS to style and position the HTML element. Here's an example code snippet:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
.container {
  position: fixed;
  bottom: 0;
  right: 0;
  max-width: 300px;
  max-height: 200px;
  background-color: #f1f1f1;
  padding: 10px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  overflow: auto;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 768px) {
  .container {
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
  }
}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
  <!-- Your content goes here -->
</div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, the HTML element is placed inside a container with the class "container". The container is styled with the following CSS properties:

- `position: fixed;` - ensures the element stays fixed in its position even when the page is scrolled.
- `bottom: 0; right: 0;` - positions the element in the bottom right corner.
- `max-width: 300px; max-height: 200px;` - limits the size of the container to a specific viewport range. Adjust these values to your desired size.
- `background-color: #f1f1f1; padding: 10px;` - adds some styling to the container.
- `box-sizing: border-box;` - includes padding and border in the element's total width and height.
- `overflow: auto;` - adds scrollbars to the container if its content exceeds the maximum height defined.

The `@media` query inside the CSS targets screens with a maximum width of 768px (adjust this value as needed). It overrides the container's max-width and max-height properties, making it take up 100% of the viewport width and height.

