### Instruction:
Use a CSS code snippet to make a div element into a circle with a given radius. However, the radius should be dynamically set based on the width of the viewport, and the circle should have a border that changes color every second.

### Response:
To make a div element into a circle with a dynamically set radius based on the width of the viewport and a border that changes color every second, you can use the following CSS code snippet:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <style>
    /* Define the circle div */
    .circle {
      width: 50%;
      height: 0;
      padding-bottom: 50%; /* The height is equal to the width */
      border-radius: 50%;
      border: 5px solid;
      animation: changeColor 1s infinite alternate; /* Animate the border color */
    }

    /* Animation to change the border color */
    @keyframes changeColor {
      0% { border-color: red; }
      50% { border-color: blue; }
      100% { border-color: green; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="circle"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this code snippet, we have defined a CSS class called "circle" which is applied to the div element. The width and height of the div are set to 50% each, and the padding-bottom property is set to 50% as well. This creates a square with equal width and height, resulting in a perfect circle.

The border-radius property is set to 50%, making the square div appear as a circle. The border property is set to 5px solid, giving the circle a border with a width of 5 pixels.

To animate the border color change every second, we define a keyframe animation called "changeColor". The animation alternates between three different border colors (red, blue, and green) in a loop, with each color lasting for 50% of the animation duration (500 milliseconds in this case).

Please note that in the provided code, the circle's initial radius is set to 50% of the viewport's width. You can adjust this value to meet your specific requirements.

### Instruction:
Create a Sass mixin to create a "toast" style that includes the following features:

1. The toast should have a border with a width of 2 pixels and a color of #888.
2. The background color of the toast should be a light shade of gray (#f0f0f0).
3. The text inside the toast should be centered horizontally and vertically.
4. The font color of the text should be #555.
5. The toast should have a padding of 15 pixels on all sides.
6. The toast should have a maximum width of 400 pixels and should truncate the text with an ellipsis if it exceeds this width.
7. The toast should have a margin of 15 pixels on all sides.

In addition to the above requirements, modify the mixin to include the following optional features:

8. Allow the user to specify the border width (in pixels) and color (as a hex code) as parameters to the mixin.
9. Allow the user to specify the background color (as a hex code) and font color (as a hex code) as parameters to the mixin.
10. Allow the user to specify the padding (in pixels), maximum width (in pixels), and margin (in pixels) as parameters to the mixin.

Ensure that the mixin is flexible and can be easily reused in different contexts.

### Response:
Here is the Sass mixin that meets all the requirements and includes the optional features:

```sass
@mixin toast-style(
  $border-width: 2px, 
  $border-color: #888, 
  $background-color: #f0f0f0, 
  $font-color: #555, 
  $padding: 15px, 
  $max-width: 400px, 
  $margin: 15px
) {
  border: $border-width solid $border-color;
  background-color: $background-color;
  color: $font-color;
  text-align: center;
  padding: $padding;
  max-width: $max-width;
  margin: $margin;
  white-space: nowrap;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.toast {
  @include toast-style;
}
```

To use this mixin, simply apply the `.toast` class to the HTML element you want to style as a toast. You can also pass optional parameters to customize the style by overriding the default values:

```sass
.custom-toast {
